## Problem

Design a library management system. Members search the catalogue, borrow physical books, return them, renew loans and join a waiting list when every copy is out. Late returns are fined.

The key idea is that a *book* (a title in the catalogue) is not the same as a *copy* on the shelf. Get that right and the rest falls into place.

## Requirements

- Members borrow up to a limit set by their plan, for a fixed number of days.
- Return a book; charge a fine for every late day.
- Renew a loan, unless someone else is waiting for that title.
- If every copy is out, a member can place a hold. The first person in the queue gets the next returned copy and has 2 days to collect it.
- Several library desks work at the same time.

## Step 1: Clarify the requirements

**Candidate:** Do we track each physical copy, or just titles?

**Interviewer:** Each copy has its own barcode.

**Candidate:** Are borrowing limits the same for everyone?

**Interviewer:** No. Basic members get 3 books for 14 days; Premium get 5 books for 21 days.

**Candidate:** If every copy is out, can members wait for it?

**Interviewer:** Yes. First come, first served, with 2 days to collect.

**Candidate:** How are fines calculated and paid?

**Interviewer:** ₹10 a day for Basic, ₹5 for Premium. Record it on return; payment is separate.

**Candidate:** Can two desks lend the last copy at the same time?

**Interviewer:** They must not.

| Functional | Non-functional |
| --- | --- |
| Borrow, return, renew, place a hold | The last copy is never lent twice |
| Fines for late returns | New membership plans need no new code |
| Holds served in order | Search stays fast for a large catalogue |

## Step 2: Identify the core objects

- **Book** – a title in the catalogue (ISBN, title, author). It also keeps the waiting list.
- **BookItem** – one physical copy with a barcode and a status: available, on loan, on the hold shelf.
- **Member** and **MembershipPlan** – who is borrowing, and their rules.
- **Loan** – one member borrowing one copy, with a due date.
- **Hold** – a member waiting for a title.
- **LibraryService** – the only place that lends, returns and hands out held copies.

[![Selected design: LibraryService checks member plan limits and checks copies out and in; a Book has physical BookItems and a first-in-first-out hold queue; each copy has at most one Loan.](designs/lld-9.svg)](designs/lld-9.svg)

> **⚖️ Design choice**
> A loan is for a **copy**; a hold is for a **title**. If Asha waits for *Practical Java*, any copy will do – she should not wait for one particular barcode while another copy sits on the shelf.

## Step 3: Design the classes

### MembershipPlan and Member

Basic and Premium members behave the same; only the numbers differ. So the numbers live in a `MembershipPlan` record, and a new plan is new data, not a new subclass.

### Book and BookItem

`Book` describes the title and owns the hold queue. `BookItem` is the copy you can hold in your hand.

### Loan and Hold

A `Loan` knows its due date and how many times it was renewed. A `Hold` knows who is waiting and, once a copy is set aside, the last day to collect it.

### LibraryService

Checkout, return, renew, place hold, and a daily job that passes uncollected copies to the next person.

[![Class diagram: a Book has many BookItems and a queue of Holds; a Member has a MembershipPlan; a Loan links one BookItem to one Member with a due date; LibraryService checks out, returns, renews and places holds.](learning-figures/lld-9-classes.svg)](learning-figures/lld-9-classes.svg)

## Step 4: Write the code

### MembershipPlan and Member

```java
/** Borrowing rules. A new plan is new data, not a new class. */
record MembershipPlan(String name, int maxBooks, int loanDays,
                      int maxRenewals, long finePerDay) {
    static final MembershipPlan BASIC   = new MembershipPlan("Basic", 3, 14, 1, 10);
    static final MembershipPlan PREMIUM = new MembershipPlan("Premium", 5, 21, 2, 5);
}

record Member(String name, MembershipPlan plan) {}
```

### Book and BookItem

```java
enum ItemStatus { AVAILABLE, ON_LOAN, ON_HOLD_SHELF }

/** One physical copy with its own barcode. */
class BookItem {
    private final String barcode;
    private final Book book;
    private ItemStatus status = ItemStatus.AVAILABLE;

    BookItem(String barcode, Book book) { this.barcode = barcode; this.book = book; }
    String getBarcode()            { return barcode; }
    Book getBook()                 { return book; }
    ItemStatus getStatus()         { return status; }
    void setStatus(ItemStatus s)   { status = s; }
}

/** A title in the catalogue. Holds are per title, because any copy will do. */
class Book {
    private final String isbn, title, author;
    private final Deque<Hold> holds = new ArrayDeque<>();

    Book(String isbn, String title, String author) {
        this.isbn = isbn; this.title = title; this.author = author;
    }
    String getTitle()     { return title; }
    String getAuthor()    { return author; }
    Deque<Hold> holds()   { return holds; }
}
```

### Loan and Hold

```java
class Loan {
    private final BookItem item;
    private final Member member;
    private LocalDate dueDate;
    private int renewals;

    Loan(BookItem item, Member member, LocalDate today) {
        this.item = item; this.member = member;
        this.dueDate = today.plusDays(member.plan().loanDays());
    }
    void extend() {
        dueDate = dueDate.plusDays(member.plan().loanDays());
        renewals++;
    }
    BookItem getItem()      { return item; }
    Member getMember()      { return member; }
    LocalDate getDueDate()  { return dueDate; }
    int getRenewals()       { return renewals; }
}

class Hold {
    private final Member member;
    private LocalDate pickupBy;           // set when a copy is waiting on the shelf

    Hold(Member member) { this.member = member; }
    Member getMember()               { return member; }
    LocalDate getPickupBy()          { return pickupBy; }
    void readyUntil(LocalDate date)  { pickupBy = date; }
}
```

### LibraryService

```java
class LibraryService {
    private final Map<String, BookItem> items = new HashMap<>();
    private final Map<String, Loan> activeLoans = new HashMap<>();   // barcode -> loan
    private final Map<BookItem, Hold> heldFor = new HashMap<>();     // copies on hold shelf

    void addCopy(BookItem item) { items.put(item.getBarcode(), item); }

    synchronized Loan checkout(Member member, String barcode, LocalDate today) {
        BookItem item = items.get(barcode);
        long borrowed = activeLoans.values().stream()
                .filter(l -> l.getMember() == member).count();
        if (borrowed >= member.plan().maxBooks()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(member.name() + " has reached the limit");
        }
        Hold hold = heldFor.get(item);
        boolean waitingForThisMember = hold != null && hold.getMember() == member;
        if (item.getStatus() != ItemStatus.AVAILABLE && !waitingForThisMember) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Copy " + barcode + " is not available");
        }
        if (waitingForThisMember) heldFor.remove(item);
        Loan loan = new Loan(item, member, today);
        item.setStatus(ItemStatus.ON_LOAN);
        activeLoans.put(barcode, loan);
        return loan;
    }

    /** Returns the fine in rupees. */
    synchronized long returnBook(String barcode, LocalDate today) {
        Loan loan = activeLoans.remove(barcode);
        if (loan == null) throw new IllegalStateException("Copy is not on loan");
        long daysLate = Math.max(0, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(loan.getDueDate(), today));
        giveToNextInQueue(loan.getItem(), today);
        return daysLate * loan.getMember().plan().finePerDay();
    }

    synchronized Hold placeHold(Member member, Book book) {
        Hold hold = new Hold(member);
        book.holds().addLast(hold);
        return hold;
    }

    synchronized Loan renew(String barcode, LocalDate today) {
        Loan loan = activeLoans.get(barcode);
        if (today.isAfter(loan.getDueDate())) throw new IllegalStateException("Overdue");
        if (loan.getRenewals() >= loan.getMember().plan().maxRenewals()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("No renewals left");
        }
        if (!loan.getItem().getBook().holds().isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Someone is waiting for this book");
        }
        loan.extend();
        return loan;
    }

    /** Daily job: a copy nobody collected goes to the next person in the queue. */
    synchronized void expireHolds(LocalDate today) {
        for (Map.Entry<BookItem, Hold> e : new ArrayList<>(heldFor.entrySet())) {
            if (today.isAfter(e.getValue().getPickupBy())) {
                heldFor.remove(e.getKey());
                giveToNextInQueue(e.getKey(), today);
            }
        }
    }

    private void giveToNextInQueue(BookItem item, LocalDate today) {
        Hold next = item.getBook().holds().pollFirst();       // first come, first served
        if (next == null) {
            item.setStatus(ItemStatus.AVAILABLE);
            return;
        }
        next.readyUntil(today.plusDays(2));                    // 2 days to collect
        heldFor.put(item, next);
        item.setStatus(ItemStatus.ON_HOLD_SHELF);
    }
}
```

`giveToNextInQueue` is used by both `returnBook` and the daily expiry job. Either a waiting member gets the copy for 2 days, or the copy goes back on the shelf.

> **⚠️ Common mistake**
> Counting days late from milliseconds. Use calendar dates (`LocalDate` and `ChronoUnit.DAYS`) so midnight and daylight-saving changes cannot add or lose a day.

## Step 5: The waiting-list algorithm in action

Asha (Basic) borrows the only copy of *Practical Java* on 1 October. It is due on 15 October.

| Date | What happens | The copy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Oct | Asha borrows | On loan to Asha |
| 2 Oct | Bilal, then Chetan, place holds | Still with Asha |
| 6 Oct | Asha tries to renew | Refused – someone is waiting |
| 18 Oct | Asha returns, 3 days late | Fine ₹30. On the hold shelf for Bilal until 20 Oct |
| 19 Oct | Chetan tries to borrow it | Refused – it is Bilal's turn |
| 21 Oct | Bilal never came; the daily job runs | On the hold shelf for Chetan until 23 Oct |
| 22 Oct | Chetan (Premium) borrows | Due 12 Nov (21 days) |

![Animation: Asha borrows the only copy; Bilal and Chetan queue; Asha returns late and is fined; the copy waits for Bilal, his hold expires, and Chetan collects it.](learning-figures/lld-9-holds.gif)

`synchronized` on every `LibraryService` method makes "check and lend" one step. Two desks scanning the last copy cannot both succeed.

> **🎯 In the interview**
> With a database, lend with one conditional update: `UPDATE book_item SET status = 'ON_LOAN' WHERE barcode = ? AND status = 'AVAILABLE'`. If no row changed, another desk got there first.

## Step 6: Deep dives

### Search

Keep indexes by title words, author and ISBN. For millions of titles, use a search engine such as Elasticsearch. Search can be slightly stale – checkout always checks the copy's real status.

### Damaged or lost copies

Add `IN_REPAIR` and `LOST` statuses. A damaged return goes to repair instead of the hold shelf; the next member keeps waiting for a healthy copy.

### Notifications

"Your book is ready for pickup" and "due tomorrow" messages are **observers** of library events.

### Different fine rules

A grace period or a maximum fine becomes a `FinePolicy` strategy, chosen per plan.

## Interviewer follow-ups

**Why not `StudentMember` and `FacultyMember` subclasses?** They behave identically; only limits differ. Composition with a `MembershipPlan` keeps one `Member` class.

**What if the first person in the queue has reached their limit?** Skip them for now (or cancel their hold with a reason) and offer the copy to the next eligible member. State the rule you choose.

**Why doesn't a return wait for the fine to be paid?** The book is physically back. Record the fine and accept the book; payment can happen later and must never block the next reader.

**How would you store loans in a database?** A copy appears in many past loans, so don't make `item_id` unique. Allow only one loan per copy with `returned_on IS NULL` (a partial unique index in PostgreSQL).

## Wrap up

We separated titles from physical copies, put limits in membership plans, and made lending, returning and the waiting list fair and safe.

> **📝 Key takeaways**
> - `Book` is the title; `BookItem` is the physical copy with a barcode.
> - Limits live in a `MembershipPlan` – composition, not subclasses.
> - Holds are a first-in-first-out queue per title, with a 2-day pickup window.
> - Fines use calendar dates and are recorded on return, never blocking it.

